---
import Page from '../layouts/Page.astro';
import PixelText from '../components/PixelText.astro';

const panels = [
  { title: 'TERMINAL', x: 6, y: 12, w: 34, h: 26, kind: 'term' },
  { title: 'TERMINAL', x: 44, y: 12, w: 34, h: 26, kind: 'term' },
  { title: 'BROWSER', x: 6, y: 44, w: 72, h: 42, kind: 'browser' },
];

// Must stay a manifest that CALM's parser accepts.
const snippetManifest = `{ "id": "standup", "name": "Standup", "version": "0.1.0",
  "entry": "index.html", "defaultSize": { "width": 480, "height": 360 },
  "contributes": { "menus": [
    { "id": "new", "title": "New standup here", "location": "canvas" }
  ] } }`;

const snippetScript = `await calm.canvas.createPanel({ kind: "terminal" });
const prs = await calm.github.api("/search/issues?q=is:pr+is:open");`;

---

<Page>

<main>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="hero-canvas" aria-hidden="true">
      <div class="canvas-grid"></div>
      {panels.map((p) => (
        <div class="canvas-panel" style={`left:${p.x}%;top:${p.y}%;width:${p.w}%;height:${p.h}%`}>
          <div class="panel-bar">
            <span class="panel-dots"></span>
            <span class="panel-title">{p.title}</span>
          </div>
          <div class:list={['panel-body', p.kind]}>
            {p.kind === 'term' ? (
              <>
                <span class="prompt">~ cargo run -p calm-app</span>
                <span class="dim">building calm-app v0.1.0</span>
                <span class="dim">Finished in 4.21s</span>
                <span class="prompt">~ <span class="cursor"></span></span>
              </>
            ) : (
              <>
                <span class="urlbar">github.com/kolezka/CALM</span>
                <span class="dim">CALM — infinite canvas for macOS</span>
              </>
            )}
          </div>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>

    <div class="hero-copy">
      <h1 class="sr-only">CALM: an infinite canvas for terminals, browsers and plugins</h1>
      <img
        class="hero-banner"
        src="/logo-animated.svg"
        width="356"
        height="132"
        alt="CALM, animated pixel wordmark"
      />
      <div class="hero-underline" aria-hidden="true"></div>
      <p class="hero-sub">
        One pan and zoom canvas on macOS. Ghostty terminals, a live browser and your own
        plugins side by side, all of them still running while you move around. A plugin
        SDK lets you shape it into whatever workspace you need.
      </p>
      <div class="hero-actions">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="https://github.com/kolezka/CALM">Source on GitHub</a>
      </div>
      <p class="hero-note">
        Early build. No release yet, so you clone it and build from source.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="buyer-beware" aria-labelledby="wrong">
    <h2 id="wrong" class="sr-only">What CALM is not</h2>
    <div class="care-box">
      <p class="care-lead">Wrong tool if you want</p>
      <ul>
        <li>a tabbed terminal, because CALM is one canvas instead</li>
        <li>a sandbox for untrusted plugins, because plugins run as trusted code</li>
        <li>a shipped download, because there is no release yet</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="features" aria-labelledby="what">
    <h2 id="what">What it does</h2>
    <div class="cards">
      <article class="card">
        <h3>Terminals</h3>
        <p>
          Live Ghostty surfaces. Five, ten, twenty of them at any size. A panel keeps its
          process through pan, zoom, preview and minimize. Only an explicit, confirmed
          close ends it.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>Browsers</h3>
        <p>
          Real web pages in a panel, with an address bar, back, forward and reload. Type a
          host and it opens, type anything else and it searches.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>Plugins</h3>
        <p>
          Panels written in HTML and JavaScript, talking to the app over <code>window.calm</code>.
          They add entries to the right-click Add menu. TSX is optional.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>TUI apps</h3>
        <p>
          One manifest turns any terminal program into an Add menu entry, so a TUI tool
          lands on the canvas like a panel instead of a window.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="extend" aria-labelledby="extend-title">
    <h2 id="extend-title">Make it yours</h2>
    <p class="extend-lead">
      CALM is a small core with a plugin SDK on top. What the canvas does not do yet, a
      plugin can add. The welcome page and the GitHub PRs panel are plugins built the same
      way yours would be.
    </p>
    <pre class="snippet" aria-label="Example plugin manifest and API call"><code><span class="dim">// calm-plugin.json</span>
{snippetManifest}

<span class="dim">// index.html</span>
{snippetScript}</code></pre>
    <div class="cards">
      <article class="card">
        <h3>A plugin is a folder</h3>
        <p>
          A manifest and an HTML page. No build step, or TSX built with bun when you want
          types and components.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>18 host calls</h3>
        <p>
          <code>window.calm</code> reaches settings, storage, its own panel, the canvas,
          <code>process.exec</code>, the theme and key bindings. <code>gh api</code> runs on
          the host, so plugin code never sees a token.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>Menus and TUI apps</h3>
        <p>
          Add items to the right-click menus. Turn any terminal program into a panel with
          one manifest: title, icon, size, colors, palette and restart.
        </p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <h3>Typed and MIT</h3>
        <p>
          TypeScript types for the whole API and starter templates, MIT licensed, so your
          plugin can use any license.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <p class="extend-note">
      Plugins run as trusted code with full access. Of the plugin events, only menu commands
      are delivered today.
      <a href="https://github.com/kolezka/CALM/blob/main/docs/plugins.md">Read the plugin guide</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="why" aria-labelledby="why-title">
    <h2 id="why-title">Why a canvas instead of windows</h2>
    <p>
      Stacked windows and tabs hide each other. A canvas does not. Draw a rectangle and you
      get a panel there. Arrange them like notes on a whiteboard, zoom out to see the whole
      workspace, zoom in to work. Zooming scales the view and never the terminal, so rows
      and columns stay put.
    </p>
    <p>
      Panels dropped edge to edge join into one block with a single divider. Distant panels
      become still previews refreshed every two seconds, which is what keeps twenty live
      terminals affordable to paint.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="discover" aria-labelledby="discover-title">
    <h2 id="discover-title">Plugins</h2>
    <p>
      A plugin gallery is planned but not built yet. CALM ships with Hello World, a GitHub PRs
      panel and a <code>top</code> TUI example, and the plugin guide shows how to write your own.
    </p>
    <a class="btn btn-primary" href="https://github.com/kolezka/CALM/blob/main/docs/plugins.md">Plugin guide</a>
  </section>

</main>

<footer class="foot">
  <PixelText text="CALM" unit={4} gap={1} fill="#455A88" label="CALM" />
  <p>Built with Rust, GPUI and AppKit. macOS 14 or newer on Apple Silicon.</p>
</footer>

</Page>
